! mcp_bootstrap_p179.strap -- MCP's TAPE/REEL DISPATCH CHAIN AND THE
! "Z ID" REEL-MOUNT ROUTINE, printed page 179.
!
! docs/MCP_Mar63_001-249.pdf PDF p.186.  The header was READ, not
! assumed, at 400 dpi over the full page width:
!
!   "TIME CLOCK 00054041   PUNID 11D11MCP   CARD ID LA000111
!    DIST. OF 3/01   3/01   PAGE NUMBER  179"
!
! >>> THE COLUMN-HEADING ROW'S OWN "LOCATION" FIELD READS 220776. <<<
! Six digits, clear of the dashed rule, printed after the second
! "LOCATION" at the right-hand end of the heading line.  That is LINE
! 1'S location word (docs/transcription_workflow.md step 1), and line
! 1 here IS a located row (220776.40), which is the case the rule
! predicts to be numbered rather than blank.  Nothing was taken from
! the survey OCR and nothing was invented off the rule.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! WHAT THE PAGE IS -- AND IT IS *NOT* A DISPATCH **TABLE**
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! FIFTY-SIX PRINTED LINES, 220776.40 .. 221040.00, CONTIGUOUS, no
! hole of any kind: no DR/DRZ reservation, no SLC (forward or
! otherwise), no DD, no comment card, no asterisk banner.  Every one
! of the 56 lines carries a location and emits bits.
!
! The brief that commissioned this page said "several landed decks
! treat pp.178-179 together as the dispatch tables, so expect
! table-shaped content".  MEASURED, THAT IS WRONG FOR p.179.  There
! is no table on this page.  What there is:
!
!   lines  1- 3  the TAIL of a branch vector that begins on p.178
!                (B,RODD / B,RECC / B,RNOECC), three half-word
!                unconditional branches and nothing else;
!   lines  4-13  four short handlers, each with its own NAME in the
!                NAME column -- K MCP (4-7), Z IOD H (8-10),
!                K UNA (11-12), K NO GO (13).  Not a table either:
!                the four are 4, 3, 2 and 1 rows long;
!   lines 14-23  a dispatch CHAIN, not a table: five pairs of
!                "KVI,3,<request code>" followed by
!                "BXE,<handler>", i.e. compare-and-branch-if-equal
!                run out straight-line (codes 2.0, 2.32, D CHEX,
!                D FREE, DIODEF).  THAT READING IS FROM PRIMARY
!                SOURCES, NOT FROM THE SHAPE: Reference Manual
!                22-6530-2 names KVI "Compare Value Immediate" and
!                says "the address part, bits 0-18, of the
!                instruction is compared with bits 0-18 of the
!                value field of the index word specified by J",
!                and its indicator table gives 53 XE "Index Equal
!                ... reset by index comparison".  Those two
!                sentences also EXPLAIN this page's printed 6-bit
!                offset field on every index-immediate row: the
!                address is only 19 bits, so bits 19-23 hold J and
!                the printed field is 2*J plus the one address bit
!                (18) that survives -- which is why line 16's
!                "KVI,3,2.32" prints .47 and line 14's "KVI,3,2.0"
!                prints .07, the only difference being the 32.
!                Lines 4-5 are a sixth pair of the
!                same shape, and the chain continues BACKWARD onto
!                p.178, whose line 42 the volume OCR indexes as
!                "220726.40 * 000013.47 04 K NI KVI,3,11.32
!                 -TEST IF WAIT, CHEX OR S FREE";
!   lines 24-41  reel/mount handling -- multi-unit and mount bit
!                tests, a time-lag subtraction against S RMTC, a
!                reminder-message switch, the current-reel walk;
!   lines 42-56  the "Z ID" routine proper, with three SIC/B
!                calling sequences and their parameter words.
!
! A table would repeat ONE shape (printed p.176 is the real article:
! twenty-four identical "SIC,STIC; BD,SIPT+n." words).  This page
! never repeats a shape more than twice running.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! >>> A SIC AND ITS BRANCH ARE ONE CARD, AND THE CARD-SERIAL COLUMN
! >>> PROVES IT ON THIS PAGE.  <<<
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! The serials run LA000111 (line 1) .. LA000162 (line 56): 52 cards
! for 56 listing lines.  FOUR serials print TWICE, and each duplicate
! pair is exactly a SIC followed by its half-word branch:
!
!   LA000119   lines  9/10   SIC,S DISIC ; B,S DISP
!   LA000156   lines 47/48   SIC,Z ID09X ; B,Z ID09
!   LA000159   lines 51/52   SIC,KCONVR  ; B,KCONV
!   LA000161   lines 54/55   SIC,S COMIC ; B,S COMM
!
! That is a MEASUREMENT that STRAP's "SIC,X; B,Y" one-card spelling
! (which decks/mcp_bootstrap_p176.strap already writes, and which
! strap_asm already enforces) is how these cards were really punched,
! not a convention the decks adopted for convenience.  The serial
! column is the witness and it costs one crop to read.
!
! Nothing fell off either end: line 1 carries the FIRST serial of the
! run and line 56 the LAST, and the heading row's LOCATION field
! independently names line 1's word.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! >>> LINE 56 IS NOT AN ORPHANED SIC.  IT IS A NULL OPERATION. <<<
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! Line 56 prints, at 1200 dpi, with a BLANK operation field:
!
!   56*  221040.00      221050.00 80                   ,Z ID01
!
! and its own card serial (LA000162), so it is not the second
! statement of a semicolon card.  strap_asm already implements this
! and cites it: "A statement with a BLANK operation field -- the
! parameter half words of a SIC/B calling sequence, printed p.133
! lines 44-45", and C22-6531 printed p.7's NULL FIELD rule ("A MAJOR
! FIELD MAY BE NULL ... if nothing but the comma denoting the field
! termination is written").  A null operation emits the
! address-and-index half word every full-word format begins with --
! 0x80 in the byte, the full 24-bit BIT address above it.
!
! So line 56 is the PARAMETER WORD of the "SIC,S COMIC; B,S COMM"
! call on lines 54/55, exactly as line 53's "VF,Z ID 02A" is the
! parameter of the call on lines 51/52 and lines 42/43/44 are the
! three parameter fields of the "LVI,15,$+1.0 / B,SA6IQS" call on
! lines 40/41.  No SIC on this page is unpaired and no branch is
! missing.
!
! BECAUSE THE NULL OPERATION DEPOSITS THE FULL 24-BIT BIT ADDRESS,
! LINE 56 IS A BIT-EXACT READING OF Z ID01, not a half-word one.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! WHAT THIS PAGE PINS TO THE BIT (and what it does not)
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! docs/transcription_workflow.md: "before writing 'the bit offset is
! unobservable', look for a SIC".  There are FOUR SICs here plus the
! null operation plus three VFs, and every one of them is a reading
! of its operand's FULL bit address:
!
!   line  9  SIC,S DISIC   -> S DISIC  = 217451.40   bit-exact
!   line 47  SIC,Z ID09X   -> Z ID09X  = 221045.00   bit-exact
!   line 51  SIC,KCONVR    -> KCONVR   = 221350.00   bit-exact
!   line 54  SIC,S COMIC   -> S COMIC  = 215470.40   bit-exact
!   line 56  ,Z ID01       -> Z ID01   = 221050.00   bit-exact
!   line 44  VF,Z ID 04    -> Z ID 04  = 221056.10   bit-exact
!   line 53  VF,Z ID 02A   -> Z ID 02A = 221053.00   bit-exact
!
! Z ID 04 at 221056.10 is NOT half-word aligned, and that is not a
! misreading: the volume OCR indexes its DEFINING card on another
! page as "20*  221056.10   Z ID04  (ITQSX)DD(BU),JOB X" -- an
! entry-mode DD that landed mid-word.  (The volume OCR is used here
! only as an INDEX to a page nobody has opened, never as a witness
! for p.179 itself.)
!
! EVERYTHING ELSE IS HALF-WORD ONLY.  A branch, an LVI, an LV, a V+,
! an SV and a BB/BZB all collapse every bit-offset below 32 to the
! same encoding, so the ".0" written on those SYNs below is a
! CONVENTION and not a reading.  Each SYN says which it is.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! TWENTY-FIVE EXTERNALS WERE ALREADY IN THE MERGED BODY IMAGE AND
! EVERY ONE OF THEM LANDS ON THIS PAGE'S PRINTED BITS
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! The pp.99-176 merge was built and its symbol table read BEFORE the
! !CHECK rows below were written.  Twenty-five of this page's
! operands already had a value there, and all twenty-five reproduce
! p.179's printed BINARY OUTPUT digit for digit:
!
!   RODD 220335.40  RECC 220340.00  RNOECC 220342.00  K NI 220726.40
!   S BA MCP 215003.00  S DISIC 217451.40  S DISP 217372.00
!   S COMIC 215470.40  S COMM 215470.00  KCONVR 221350.00
!   KCONV 221335.00  Z ID09 221042.00  Z ID09X 221045.00
!   S IOD I 010004.00  S FREE 12.32  D FREE 10.32  D CHEX 12.0
!   DIODEF 13.0  S OWNER .32  S IMNT .53  S MULTI .27
!   S MOUNT .51  S RMIND 1.63  S CREEL 8.32  S RMTC 1.32
!
! The SYN cards below are COPIED from the landed decks that carry
! them (SYN cards, not PLACE cards -- the thing printed p.157 got
! wrong was copying an "XW", which deposits at the copier's own
! counter; a SYN deposits nothing and is checked against the
! defining page by the merge).  Their data descriptions are carried
! with them, which is the pp.153-160 rule.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! TWO GLYPHS THE INK COULD NOT SETTLE, SETTLED BY THE ASSEMBLER AND
! THE COMMENT COLUMN TOGETHER
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! LINE 29 reads "BZAF" or "BZAH" at 1200 dpi.  Measured over every
! B<indicator> / BZ<indicator> / B<indicator>Z / BZ<indicator>Z
! spelling strap_asm accepts, exactly ONE produces this row's printed
! "221041.37 40":
!
!     BZAH,KCHOPN   ->  221041.37 40     (AH = indicator 62)
!     BZAF,KCHOPN   ->  "unknown mnemonic 'BZAF'"
!     BZAE,KCHOPN   ->  221041.36 C0
!     BZAL,KCHOPN   ->  221041.36 40
!
! and the row's own comment is "-BRANCH IF TIME NOT ELAPSED", with
! line 27 immediately above it subtracting S RMTC into the
! accumulator.  Branch-on-ACCUMULATOR-HIGH is what that computes.
! Eliminator AND semantics agree, so this one is closed.
!
! LINE 30 reads "BBI" or "BB1".  "BBI" is not a mnemonic; "BB1" is
! Branch on Bit and Set to One (opcodes.py BB_CODES, mod=0b11), whose
! second half word's byte is 0b0000_11_1_0 = 0x0E -- which is what
! the page prints, where a plain BB prints 02 and a BZB prints 00.
! The comment column closes it independently: "-BR. IF REMINDER
! SENT,SET SWTCH".  SET.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THE LOCATION-COLUMN ASTERISK
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THREE rows carry the second asterisk, in the LOCATION column after
! the bit offset: lines 13 (221004.40), 30 (221020.00) and 48
! (221033.40).  Recorded, not explained.  No rule is proposed from
! one page -- docs/transcription_workflow.md records that the mark
! now has eight things it is NOT, and that printed p.147's proposed
! rule was refuted by p.151 the same afternoon.  For the record, the
! three rows are a BB, a BB1 and a B; two of them (13, 48) are the
! first half word of a full-word instruction and one (30) is not, so
! "every full-word instruction" is already dead on this page alone.
!
! Separately: EVERY line number on this page is followed by an
! asterisk ("1*", "2*", ... "56*").  That is NOT the location-column
! mark and is NOT a p.179 peculiarity -- the volume OCR shows the
! same glyph after the line number on pages all over the listing
! (e.g. "20* 220335.40", "21* 220715.40", "42* 220726.40").  It is a
! column separator.  Non-finding, written down so the next
! transcriber does not spend a crop on it.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THE SURVEY IS WRONG AT BOTH ENDS
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! Cached survey: 220777.0 .. 221027.00.
! Measured:      220776.40 .. 221040.00, 56 lines, 52 cards.
! It lost line 1 at the top and the last SEVEN rows (lines 50-56) at
! the bottom.  The card-serial run LA000111..LA000162 is what proves
! neither end is a crop artefact.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! JOINING THIS PAGE TO THE BODY IMAGE
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! This deck was written under a scope rule that forbids writing to
! tests/, so the registration was NOT made.  When pp.177 and 178
! land, tests/check_mcp_body_image.py's "FIRST, LAST" becomes
! 99, 179 and its body_page_count check becomes 80.  (NOT
! strap/mcp_image.py's MCP_IPL_PAGES -- pp.15-98 and pp.99+ are two
! assemblies and merging them fails on SMCP.)  The three pages are
! interdependent; 179 cannot be added while 177/178 are missing.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! WHAT THE NEXT PAGE MUST PRINT -- A FALSIFIABLE PREDICTION
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! The last row is line 56 at 221040.00 and it is a HALF WORD (a null
! operation emits one half word).  So THE COUNTER LEAVES THIS PAGE AT
! 221040.40, and printed p.180's line 1 must be located there.
!
! Four of this page's own forward references land on p.180 and are
! further predictions its transcriber can check against their own
! LOCATION column, with no reference to this deck:
!
!   KCHOPN   221041.00   (lines 24,25,29,30 -- half-word only)
!   Z ID09   221042.00   (line 48; already SYN'd by p.166's deck)
!   Z ID09X  221045.00   (line 47 -- BIT-EXACT, it is a SIC)
!   Z ID15   221045.40   (line 45 -- half-word only)
!   Z ID13   221047.00   (line 49 -- half-word only)
!   Z ID01   221050.00   (line 56 -- BIT-EXACT, null operation)
!   Z ID 02A 221053.00   (line 53 -- BIT-EXACT, a VF)
!   Z ID03   221055.00   (line 39 -- half-word only)
!   Z ID 04  221056.10   (line 44 -- BIT-EXACT, a VF; note the .10)
!   Z ID04A  221060.00   (line 46 -- half-word only)
!   Z ID14   221063.00   (line 36 -- half-word only)
!   Z RENME  221064.40   (line 37 -- half-word only)
!   K NOT AV 221066.00   (line 13 -- half-word only)
!
! And one BACKWARD: p.178's last row must be at 220776.00, because
! this page's line 1 is at 220776.40.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! SYMBOLS THIS PAGE DEFINES (the NAME column, nine of them)
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
!   K MCP    221000.00   line  4
!   Z IOD H  221002.00   line  8   (comment "-INVALID IOD REF NO"
!                                   is what settles the O and the D)
!   K UNA    221003.40   line 11
!   K NO GO  221004.40   line 13
!   Z ID06   221023.00   line 34
!   Z ID 10  221030.00   line 42
!   Z ID09A  221031.40   line 45
!   Z ID08   221035.00   line 50
!   Z ID07   221035.40   line 51
!
! STRAP ignores blanks inside a name (C22-6531 printed p.10: a name
! is alphameric), and strap_asm refuses a blank in the LABEL field,
! so every name is written closed up here, as every landed deck
! does.  The printed spacing is recorded in the listing block.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! WHAT THIS PAGE COULD NOT SETTLE
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! * Z RMTCC's LENGTH AND LOW BITS.  Line 28 is "KI(BU,6),Z RMTCC"
!   and prints 770000.00 in the immediate half word.  An immediate
!   is LEFT-JUSTIFIED in the 24-bit A24 field and the STATEMENT
!   writes its own (BU,6), so this row pins the TOP SIX BITS of the
!   value to 111111 and NOTHING ELSE -- not the symbol's own length,
!   not any bit below the sixth.  The volume OCR indexes a defining
!   card on another page as "Z RMTCC  CCI(BU,6),(2)111111", but the
!   MNEMONIC in that OCR line is garbled ("CCI" is not an operation)
!   and it is therefore not safe to read the dds off it.  The SYN
!   below writes (BU,6),(2)111111, which reproduces the printed word;
!   THE DDS IS UNVERIFIED, AND THE MERGE CANNOT CATCH IT.  Z RMTCC
!   is EXTERNAL on every page of pp.99-179 -- no landed page defines
!   it -- so a green body-image build is SILENT here, not
!   confirming.  Do not read the merge passing as evidence for this
!   one card.  What would settle it: the page that prints Z RMTCC's
!   defining card, read at 1200 dpi.
!
! * THE OPEN (BU) QUESTION FROM pp.169-176 IS NOT SETTLED HERE, and
!   cannot be.  Every data description on this page writes an
!   explicit length -- (BU,26) twice, (BU,6) once -- and there is no
!   mode-only dds anywhere on the page.  Next transcriber: do not
!   re-check p.179 for this.
!
! * LINE 1'S OPERAND IS WRITTEN "RODD" AND THIS PAGE'S INK SAYS
!   "RCDD".  THE INK LOSES, AND IT IS WORTH SAYING WHY.  Rendered at
!   1800 dpi the second glyph is a round form with a clear break in
!   its upper right -- a C, to the eye, exactly the way printed
!   p.87's "T WORM" presented as "T WCRM".  The first draft of this
!   deck therefore wrote RCDD and
!   tests/check_symbol_spellings.py REFUSED IT:
!
!     FAIL two spellings of one symbol at 4732768:
!          RCDD (pp.179) vs RODD (pp.167) -- glyph C/O
!
!   decks/mcp_bootstrap_p167.strap carries the DEFINING card at
!   220335.40 -- the same bit this page's line 1 branches to -- and
!   names it RODD from C22-6728-1 printed p.271's IOD attribute
!   table (ODD / EVEN / ECC / HD / LD), a TYPESET source, which is
!   rung 2 of docs/transcription_workflow.md's glyph ladder and
!   beats ink outright.  p.167 also records that the DEFINING card's
!   own ink reads "RCCD", the same dropout in the same position on a
!   different page.  And the asymmetry runs one way: dropout can
!   open a closed glyph but never closes an open one, so an observed
!   C is consistent with an O while an observed O could never be a
!   C.  Three independent reasons, all pointing the same way.
!
!   The volume OCR's hit on the defining card ("RCDD") is NOT a
!   counterweight: it is tesseract on the same worn glyph, the
!   weakest reader of the two, and p.167's transcriber read that
!   card's ink directly.
!
!   RECORDED AS A LIVE DISAGREEMENT BETWEEN INK AND NAME so that
!   nobody re-opens it from p.179 alone.  What would close it for
!   good: a page that spells the symbol with a CLOSED O.
!
! * THE BRANCH VECTOR ON LINES 1-3 IS A TAIL AND ITS HEAD IS NOT ON
!   THIS PAGE.  decks/mcp_bootstrap_p167.strap predicted the whole
!   six-entry vector before p.178 or p.179 was opened:
!
!     220775.00  220325.10 00  B,RHD
!     220775.40  220326.10 00  B,RLD
!     220776.00  220334.50 00  B,REVEN
!     220776.40  220335.50 00  B,RODD      <-- this page's line 1
!     220777.00  220340.10 00  B,RECC      <-- line 2
!     220777.40  220342.10 00  B,RNOECC    <-- line 3
!
!   THE LAST THREE ARE NOW MEASURED OFF THE PAGE AND ALL THREE MATCH
!   DIGIT FOR DIGIT, including the 220335.50 p.167 could only write
!   as "2203?5.50" from the volume OCR.  SO THE FIRST THREE ARE A
!   PREDICTION FOR printed p.178: its last three rows must be
!   220775.00 B,RHD, 220775.40 B,RLD and 220776.00 B,REVEN, in that
!   order, and its last row must be at 220776.00.  What selects
!   among the six is still nobody's measurement.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! WHAT WAS ACTUALLY RUN, AND WHAT IT SAID
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! This deck was written in a scratchpad, not in decks/, so each
! checker was pointed at it explicitly rather than taken on trust:
!
!   verify_mcp_page.py (DECK patched to the scratchpad copy)
!       -> 9 symbols, 68 listing rows, ALL TESTS PASSED
!   check_symbol_spellings.py (DECKS patched to decks/ + this page)
!       -> 1483 symbol definitions, no symbol spelled two ways
!          (ITS FIRST RUN FAILED -- see the RODD note below; that
!           failure is the most useful thing any checker said today)
!   check_deck_listing_comments.py (same patch)
!       -> this deck's own block parses: 56 rows read, 55 compared
!          against the !CHECKs, 0 mismatches.  The one row not
!          compared is a CONVENTION mismatch and not a gap: twelve
!          of these lines print TWO half words on one listing line
!          and are checked as two separate !CHECKs, so the block's
!          second binary has no row of its own to pair with.  Every
!          one of those second half words IS checked -- see the
!          verify_mcp_page run above, which compares 68 rows to the
!          block's 56.
!   mcp_image.build(pp.99-176 + this page)
!       -> merges without a symbol or dds disagreement, and all
!          nine of this page's NAMEs land on their printed
!          locations.  (pp.177 and 178 are not in that list because
!          they do not exist yet; the real registration waits for
!          them.)
!
! EXACTLY ONE ROW FAILED on the first assembly and it was MY
! misreading, not the page's and not the assembler's: line 6's byte
! was transcribed 80 and the page prints B0.  Re-rendered at 1600
! dpi the glyph has a straight left stem and two bowls -- a B -- and
! the other V+ on this page (line 37) prints B0 as well.  Recorded
! because the B/8 pair is the first thing this drum loses and
! because the failure is what found it.
!
